# Render & publish commands

Run everything from the project folder:

    cd ~/ERH/50-Sites/rodriguez-huerta-portfolio

Preview before publishing (live reload, Ctrl+C to stop):

    quarto preview

Pages built from R code keep a cache in `_freeze/`. When you touch one of those
pages you must render it AND commit its `_freeze/` folder, otherwise GitHub
rebuilds from the old cache. Pages with code: about, publications,
conferences, blog, cv, cv-negative. Everything else — home, projects,
portfolio, individual entries — needs no render at all: commit the `.qmd` and
push.


## 1. Whole site

Use after changing `styles.css`, `_quarto.yml`, `_includes/`, or anything in `R/`.

    rm -rf _freeze
    quarto render
    git add -A
    git commit -m "Update site"
    git push

`rm -rf _freeze` is required after editing `R/` — Quarto does not notice those
edits on its own and will happily reuse the stale cache.


## 2. One section

### Publications / Conferences (data lives in cv_inputs.xlsx)

Edit the `publications` or `events` sheet, save, close Excel, then:

    quarto render publications.qmd
    quarto render conferences.qmd
    git add _freeze/publications/ _freeze/conferences/
    git commit -m "Update publications and conferences"
    git push

### CV / CV of failures (same spreadsheet)

    quarto render cv.qmd
    quarto render cv-negative.qmd
    git add _freeze/cv/ _freeze/cv-negative/ cv.pdf
    git commit -m "Update CV"
    git push

### About

    quarto render about.qmd
    git add about.qmd _freeze/about/
    git commit -m "Update about page"
    git push

### Home (no code, nothing to render)

    git add index.qmd
    git commit -m "Update home page"
    git push

### Projects and Portfolio entries (plain text, no code)

    git add projects/ portfolio/
    git commit -m "Update project text"
    git push

(The Conferences page is built from the spreadsheet, not from the
`conferences/` folder — see the Publications / Conferences block above.)


## 3. New blog post

    cp blog/_template.qmd blog/2026-08-19-my-post.qmd

Name the file `YYYY-MM-DD-short-title.qmd`. Open it, fill in title,
description, date and categories, and set `draft: false` when it is ready —
a draft never appears on the live site.

    quarto preview                 # check it looks right
    quarto render blog.qmd         # refresh the listing cache
    git add blog/2026-08-19-my-post.qmd blog.qmd _freeze/blog/
    git commit -m "New blog post: my post"
    git push

Same pattern for a new project, portfolio or conference entry — copy the
`_template` in that folder — except those listings have no code, so skip the
`quarto render` and the `_freeze/` line.


## After pushing

GitHub rebuilds and deploys in ~2 minutes. Check it went green:

    open https://github.com/edgarodriguez/rodriguez-huerta-portfolio/actions

Push rejected ("your current branch is behind")?

    git pull --rebase --autostash origin main
    git push
